1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:04,212
AGENT PIKE: Five bills. All hundreds.

2
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:09,998
AGENT PIKE: Same five serial numbers. Forty-six of each.

3
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:13,986
YOU: I can explain.

4
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:17,971
AGENT PIKE: Great. Start with the printer.

5
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:22,950
YOU: There’s no printer. There’s a wallet.

6
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:27,907
To be fair, it was a very good wallet.

7
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:42,895
This is you. Thirty-one, rent due on Friday, and three dollars short of everything.

8
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:54,002
This is your wallet, the next morning.

9
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:56,629
YOU: Wait. What?

10
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:03,344
Five hundred dollars. In hundreds. You have never even held a hundred.

11
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:06,532
YOU: Okay. Okay. Nobody panic.

12
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:13,056
So you test it, like a scientist. A nervous scientist.

13
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:19,326
Sock drawer. Full.

14
00:01:28,250 --> 00:01:31,291
Freezer. Full, and cold.

15
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:44,180
Taped shut. Full, and sticky.

16
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:48,650
Five hundred a day is three thousand five hundred a week.

17
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:54,666
A hundred and eighty-two thousand, five hundred a year. Tax-free, as far as you know.

18
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:04,776
BARISTA: Got anything smaller?

19
00:02:06,650 --> 00:02:09,286
YOU: No. And I never will.

20
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:13,435
That one, at least, was true.

21
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:33,007
Day two, the good headphones. Day three, the good chair.

22
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:36,995
Day five, a kayak. You live on the fourth floor.

23
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:41,669
And every morning, five hundred more.

24
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:11,588
YOU: This coffee used to taste like a holiday.

25
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:15,429
BARISTA: It’s the same coffee.

26
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:19,932
YOU: I know. That’s the problem.

27
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:30,450
In 1978, psychologists asked lottery winners how happy they were.

28
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:34,250
They turned out no happier than people who hadn’t won.

29
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:39,896
It’s called hedonic adaptation. The amazing becomes normal. Fast.

30
00:03:41,650 --> 00:03:46,250
By day forty, you have a new problem. You can’t spend it fast enough.

31
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:51,496
Hundreds pile up in shoe boxes. Then in the kayak.

32
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:57,903
YOU: I need a bank.

33
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:02,751
Oh, you do. And you really, really don’t.

34
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:29,006
TELLER: Good morning. Depositing?

35
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:34,588
YOU: Twenty-three thousand dollars. In cash. Normal amount.

36
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:42,137
TELLER: Over ten thousand in cash, I file a report with the government. It’s routine.

37
00:04:44,150 --> 00:04:48,414
YOU: Then do nine thousand nine hundred today, and the rest tomorrow?

38
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:56,489
TELLER: Splitting deposits to dodge the report is called structuring. That’s a crime.

39
00:04:58,450 --> 00:05:01,175
YOU: Even if the money’s real?

40
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:05,243
TELLER: Even if the money’s real.

41
00:05:06,550 --> 00:05:10,800
True, by the way. And it wasn’t even your biggest problem.

42
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:16,050
Every banknote has its own serial number. One number, one note.

43
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:21,337
Yours had the same five numbers. Forty-six times each.

44
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:45,598
Fake money in America is a job for the Secret Service. It’s what they were founded for, in 1865.

45
00:05:46,050 --> 00:05:48,402
AGENT PIKE: Sir. Bring the bag.

46
00:05:54,650 --> 00:05:57,038
AGENT PIKE: Where’s the printer?

47
00:05:58,850 --> 00:06:01,033
YOU: It’s a wallet.

48
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:04,471
AGENT PIKE: This is empty.

49
00:06:05,850 --> 00:06:08,562
YOU: It refills in the morning.

50
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:12,370
AGENT PIKE: Of course it does.

51
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:19,374
Here’s what Agent Pike was really worried about. Not you. Everyone.

52
00:06:20,050 --> 00:06:25,814
If one wallet refills, you’re rich. If every wallet refills, nobody is.

53
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,250
Everyone has more money, but there’s the same amount of bread.

54
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:34,097
So bread goes up. Then up again.

55
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:45,250
In Germany in 1923, a loaf of bread cost 200 billion marks.

56
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:50,517
In Zimbabwe in 2008, prices doubled about once a day.

57
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:58,008
Money works because there isn’t much of it. Make it endless, and it’s just paper.

58
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:07,628
YOU: So I’m a threat to the economy?

59
00:07:09,050 --> 00:07:14,068
AGENT PIKE: You? America has over twenty trillion dollars. You’re a rounding error.

60
00:07:16,650 --> 00:07:18,975
YOU: Oh. That’s good.

61
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:23,085
AGENT PIKE: You’re a threat to my paperwork.

62
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:34,291
Also, found money counts as income. A court ruled on it in 1969, about a couple who found cash inside an old piano.

63
00:07:34,450 --> 00:07:36,504
YOU: A piano?

64
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:40,619
AGENT PIKE: The wallet stays here. Evidence.

65
00:07:42,550 --> 00:07:44,525
YOU: That’s fair.

66
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:53,160
Day eighty-one. No wallet. One kayak. One tax bill.

67
00:08:10,650 --> 00:08:14,888
That night, the wallet slept in locker forty-six.

68
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:42,074
AGENT PIKE: …Form.

69
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:51,925
Agent Pike logged five hundred dollars every morning for the next three years. He never spent a cent of it.

70
00:08:52,250 --> 00:08:58,027
People said he was the most honest man in the building. Also the most tired.

71
00:09:09,650 --> 00:09:11,776
BARISTA: Got anything smaller?

72
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:16,149
YOU: Actually, yes.

73
00:09:21,650 --> 00:09:28,044
The coffee tasted like a holiday again. It was never the coffee. It was the running out.
